---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Precision Placement — Varve Docs"
  description="Use exact nudges, scoped snapping, explicit alignment references, and unambiguous distribution in Varve."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Precision Placement
      </nav>

      <h1>Precision placement</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Varve keeps deliberate movement, magnetic assistance, and one-shot arrangement separate so
        a precise command stays precise.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="nudge-title">
        <h2 id="nudge-title">Exact nudging</h2>
        <p>
          Focus the canvas with an eligible selection, then press an Arrow key. Arrow uses the
          configured small amount; <strong>Shift + Arrow</strong> uses the independently configured
          big amount. Values accept positive fractions and are stored in canonical geometry units,
          while Settings displays them in your General unit preference.
        </p>
        <p>
          Camera zoom, camera rotation, device scale, and object rotation do not change the
          requested displacement. With snapping disabled, an object at <code>x = 10.25</code>
          moved right by <code>1</code> ends at <code>x = 11.25</code>. Keyboard nudging does not
          reparent an object or apply pointer snapping.
        </p>
        <p>
          Open <a href={sitePath('/docs/settings')}>Settings</a> → Nudging &amp; Movement to edit
          the two amounts or restore their defaults. Numeric fields and the Inspector's position
          controls provide pointer-friendly alternatives to a physical keyboard.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="snap-title">
        <h2 id="snap-title">Scoped snapping</h2>
        <p>
          Pointer movement uses the current world-space pointer proposal, then applies at most one
          common correction to a multi-selection. Candidate scope is the active editing surface;
          moving roots and their dependent descendants are excluded. Current targets include
          visible object edges and centers, page/frame bounds, ruler guides, document/layout grids,
          and the explicit pixel-grid mode where enabled.
        </p>
        <p>
          Configure pointer snapping in the Inspector's <strong>Document → Snapping</strong>
          section. The tolerance is 1–32 CSS pixels, with separate switches for object geometry,
          page/frame bounds, and ruler/layout guides. <strong>Reset snapping</strong> restores
          those viewport preferences; document-grid snap and integer pixel snap remain separate
          controls in Document Grid.
        </p>
        <div class="contract-grid">
          <article>
            <h3>Magnetic assistance</h3>
            <p>Screen-space tolerance acquires a nearby eligible target and releases from raw pointer intent.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Temporary bypass</h3>
            <p>Hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> on Linux/Windows or <strong>Cmd</strong> on macOS during a drag to bypass snapping without changing the drag origin.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Explainable feedback</h3>
            <p>Transient guides retain target identity, source feature, target feature, correction, and category so a stale lock cannot describe a replacement.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p>
          Grid and pixel alignment are separate from ordinary magnetic snapping. Turning on a
          visible grid does not round all document coordinates, and moving an object with Arrow
          keys does not silently snap it to an integer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Selection handles use these same scoped line targets. With proportional resize enabled,
          a snapped edge or corner keeps the original aspect ratio and fixed opposite anchor; Varve
          never rounds width and height independently.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="arrange-title">
        <h2 id="arrange-title">Alignment and distribution</h2>
        <p>
          In the Inspector's Align &amp; Distribute bar, choose the reference before executing an
          operation: selection bounds, a containing frame, an explicit page, or a key object. The
          active reference is visible in the control, and the key object remains stationary. A
          single eligible object can still align to a page or valid frame.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Mode</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Availability</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Equal gaps</th><td>Equal edge-to-edge gaps across a fixed span; object sizes remain part of the calculation.</td><td>Three or more eligible objects</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Equal centers</th><td>Equal intervals between reference centers; it is not an equal-gap operation.</td><td>Three or more eligible objects</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Fixed gap</th><td>Places objects with the entered signed gap after stable spatial ordering.</td><td>Two or more eligible objects</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Tidy Up</th><td>One-time grid arrangement that does not create persistent auto layout.</td><td>Two or more eligible objects</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          Commands preserve dimensions, transforms, styles, parentage, and paint order. A completed
          command is one undo entry; Escape or pointer cancellation restores a spacing preview.
          Guides describe the applied result and its reference rather than a stale pre-command
          snapshot. See <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/auto-layout')}>Auto Layout</a> when children
          are intentionally flow-managed.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="focus-title">
        <h2 id="focus-title">Focus and accessibility</h2>
        <p>
          Arrow keys belong to the focused text field, number field, slider, toolbar, dialog, or
          IME composition. The canvas only owns them when it has focus. If a keyboard is not
          available, use the numeric Position &amp; Size controls, Align &amp; Distribute actions, or
          fixed-gap field; these paths do not require a drag.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the complete current bindings and platform modifier labels, see
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/keyboard-shortcuts')}>Keyboard Shortcuts</a>. For grid origins,
          subdivisions, and pixel-grid semantics, see <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/grids')}>Grid Systems</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <aside class="docs-note">
        <strong>Current boundary:</strong> visual/effect bounds, typography baselines, path-anchor
        snapping, and responsive absolute-child anchors are not presented here as complete
        precision-placement modes. They need their own metric and UI contracts before being
        advertised.
      </aside>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: .875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .docs-page h1 { color: var(--text-primary); font: 700 clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.8rem)/1.1 var(--font-editorial); letter-spacing: -.02em; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .docs-intro { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 1.18rem; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .docs-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .docs-section h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.55rem; margin-bottom: .9rem; }
  .docs-section h3 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1rem; margin: 0; }
  .docs-section p { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  .docs-section a { color: var(--text-link); }
  code { background: var(--surface-muted); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--website-radius-control-compact); color: var(--text-primary); padding: .1rem .3rem; }
  .contract-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  .contract-grid article { background: var(--surface-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-surface); padding: var(--space-4); }
  .contract-grid p { font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.55; margin: .5rem 0 0; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 1.25rem 0; width: 100%; }
  th, td { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); padding: .75rem; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
  th { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .docs-note { background: var(--surface-muted); border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-surface); color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.65; padding: var(--space-4); }
  .docs-note strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  @media (max-width: 680px) {
    .docs-page { padding: 2rem 0 4rem; }
    .contract-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    table { font-size: .9rem; }
    th, td { padding: .6rem .45rem; }
  }
</style>
